  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="hub"><div class="hub-box"><div class="hub-title">ASC 505 — Equity</div><div class="hub-sub">Extended reference poster · US GAAP · Subtopics: 505-10 Overall, 505-20 Stock Dividends/Splits, 505-30 Treasury Stock, 505-60 Spinoffs · USD examples</div></div></div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci"><div class="ctitle ci">The residual claim, and the transactions that move it around</div><div class="principle">Equity is the residual interest in an entity's assets after deducting liabilities — but a rich variety of TRANSACTIONS move value between its components (stock dividends, splits, treasury stock, spinoffs, ordinary cash dividends) without necessarily changing TOTAL equity itself. ASC 505 governs how each of these specific transaction types is recognised and presented.</div></div>
      <div class="card cs"><div class="ctitle cs">Several genuinely distinctive US GAAP mechanics</div><div class="principle">ASC 505 contains some of the more MECHANICALLY DETAILED, distinctly US GAAP-specific rules in the whole Codification — the small-stock-dividend fair-value capitalisation rule (Section 3) and the carrying-amount-based spinoff treatment (Section 6, contrasted with IFRIC 17's fair-value approach) are both worth knowing well.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A broad range of equity transactions</div><div class="principle">ASC 505 applies to stock dividends and splits, treasury stock transactions, ordinary dividend declarations, spinoffs and reverse spinoffs, and equity-based payments to non-employees — governing HOW each is recognised and presented, working alongside ASC 215's presentation framework and ASC 480's liability/equity classification threshold.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Stock Dividends vs Stock Splits — a Fair-Value Capitalisation Quirk</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A distinctly US-specific mechanical rule</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">A "SMALL" stock dividend (generally under 20–25% of previously outstanding shares) requires CAPITALISING retained earnings at the FAIR VALUE of the shares issued — transferring an amount equal to the fair market value of the new shares from retained earnings into common stock/APIC. A "LARGE" stock dividend, or a stock split effected in dividend FORM (generally 20–25% or more), requires capitalising only PAR VALUE (or, for a pure stock split, simply a memo adjustment to share count and par value per share, with no formal capitalisation entry at all). <strong>IFRS has NO equivalent fair-value-based capitalisation quirk</strong> — a bonus issue or stock split under IFRS is generally treated as simply increasing share count, with NO fair-value-based transfer between equity components required regardless of size, making this a genuinely distinctive, detailed US GAAP-specific mechanical rule.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Treasury Stock — the Cost Method</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Converged in substance with IAS 32</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Repurchased shares are most commonly accounted for under the COST METHOD — recorded as a CONTRA-EQUITY account at REPURCHASE COST, with any gain or loss on later REISSUANCE recognised WITHIN EQUITY (typically APIC or retained earnings), NEVER through net income. (A less common PAR VALUE method exists too, recording treasury stock at par with the excess charged to APIC/retained earnings.) This "never through profit or loss" principle is FULLY CONVERGED with IAS 32, which explicitly prohibits recognising any gain or loss in profit or loss on the purchase, sale, issuance, or cancellation of an entity's own equity instruments.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Dividends — Recognised on Declaration</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">A liability arises at declaration, not payment</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">A dividend is recognised as a REDUCTION of retained earnings (and a liability to shareholders) when DECLARED by the board of directors — not when actually PAID. This timing is broadly converged with IFRS practice, where a dividend declared AFTER the reporting period is treated as a NON-ADJUSTING subsequent event under IAS 10, disclosed but not recognised as a liability at the prior period end — the same fundamental "recognise upon declaration, not upon an earlier intention" philosophy.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Spinoffs (505-60) — Recorded at Carrying Amount</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A genuine, significant divergence from IFRIC 17</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">A SPINOFF — a distribution of a subsidiary's shares to the parent's own shareholders — is generally recorded at the SUBSIDIARY'S CARRYING AMOUNT (its book value), NOT fair value, reducing retained earnings by that carrying amount, with NO gain or loss recognised on the distribution itself. <strong>IFRIC 17</strong> (Distributions of Non-cash Assets to Owners) instead requires the distributed asset to be measured at FAIR VALUE, with any DIFFERENCE between fair value and carrying amount recognised as a GAIN OR LOSS in profit or loss at the distribution date. This is a genuinely significant divergence: a spinoff of an appreciated subsidiary produces NO income statement impact under US GAAP, but a real recognised GAIN under IFRS for the identical underlying transaction.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Equity-Based Payments to Non-Employees</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Aligned with ASC 718 since a 2018 internal US simplification</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">ASU 2018-07 extended ASC 718's EMPLOYEE-based stock compensation measurement principles to NON-EMPLOYEE awards as well (e.g. equity granted to consultants or vendors) — a simplification WITHIN US GAAP itself, aligning what had previously been a separate measurement model (505-50) with the mainstream employee stock compensation guidance, rather than a convergence point with IFRS specifically (IFRS 2 has generally applied a broadly similar unified approach to employee and non-employee share-based payments all along).</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — Small Stock Dividend Capitalisation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Capitalising retained earnings at fair value</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A 10% stock dividend</div><div class="wk-body">An entity has 1,000,000 shares outstanding, trading at $20 per share. The board declares a 10% (small) stock dividend, issuing 100,000 new shares. Par value is $1 per share.</div><div class="wk-answer">Since 10% is a SMALL stock dividend, capitalise at FAIR VALUE: 100,000 shares × $20 = $2,000,000 transferred from retained earnings. Of that, $100,000 (100,000 × $1 par) goes to common stock, and the remaining $1,900,000 goes to APIC: Debit Retained earnings $2,000,000 / Credit Common stock $100,000 / Credit APIC $1,900,000.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Treasury Stock Reissuance</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A gain on reissuance, recognised within equity</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Reissuing repurchased shares above cost</div><div class="wk-body">An entity previously repurchased 10,000 shares of treasury stock at $15 per share ($150,000 total). It later reissues those shares at $22 per share ($220,000 total).</div><div class="wk-answer">Debit Cash $220,000 / Credit Treasury stock $150,000 / Credit APIC — treasury stock transactions $70,000 — the $70,000 "gain" is recognised WITHIN EQUITY (APIC), NEVER through net income, consistent with both the US cost method and IAS 32's parallel prohibition on recognising such gains in profit or loss.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example — a Spinoff at Carrying Amount</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Contrasting the US and IFRS outcomes</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Distributing an appreciated subsidiary's shares</div><div class="wk-body">A parent distributes 100% of a subsidiary's shares to its own shareholders as a spinoff. The subsidiary's carrying amount (net assets) is $40,000,000; its estimated fair value is $95,000,000.</div><div class="wk-answer"><strong>Under US GAAP:</strong> Debit Retained earnings $40,000,000 / Credit Investment in subsidiary $40,000,000 — NO gain recognised, despite the $55,000,000 difference between fair value and carrying amount.<br><strong>Under IFRIC 17:</strong> the distribution is measured at the $95,000,000 FAIR VALUE, with a <strong>$55,000,000 GAIN</strong> recognised in profit or loss for the difference between fair value and the $40,000,000 carrying amount — a materially different income statement outcome for the identical underlying corporate action.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 505 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Description of each class of stock, including par value, shares authorised/issued/outstanding</span><span>Dividends declared per share and in total, by class</span><span>Nature and accounting treatment of stock dividends, splits, and treasury stock transactions during the period</span><span>Nature and carrying amount of assets distributed in a spinoff</span></div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether a stock distribution qualifies as a "small" (fair value) or "large" (par value) dividend under the 20–25% threshold convention</span><span>Whether a specific transaction qualifies as a genuine spinoff versus another form of distribution</span></div></div>
      <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Fair value of shares issued in a small stock dividend</span><span>Carrying amount of net assets distributed in a spinoff</span></div></div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>ASC 505 vs IAS 32 / IFRIC 17 — Comprehensive Comparison</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASC 505 vs the IFRS equivalents</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP (ASC 505)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS equivalent</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Small stock dividend capitalisation</span><span>Fair value transferred from retained earnings</span><span>No equivalent fair-value capitalisation rule for bonus issues/splits under IFRS</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Treasury stock gains/losses</span><span>Recognised within equity, never net income (cost method)</span><span>Same principle under IAS 32 — fully converged</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Dividend recognition timing</span><span>At declaration</span><span>At declaration, with post-period-end declarations as non-adjusting IAS 10 events</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Spinoff measurement</span><span>CARRYING AMOUNT — no gain/loss recognised</span><span>FAIR VALUE under IFRIC 17 — gain/loss recognised for the difference</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note">The spinoff measurement divergence is the standout difference in this Topic — worth cross-referencing against the IFRIC 17 poster elsewhere in this library for the full IFRS-side detail.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 505 event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Small stock dividend</span><span><span class="badge be">SE</span></span><span>Retained earnings capitalised at fair value into common stock/APIC</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Treasury stock reissuance gain/loss</span><span><span class="badge be">SE</span></span><span>Never flows through net income</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Dividend declaration</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge be">SE</span></span><span>Reduces retained earnings; recognises a liability until paid</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Spinoff distribution</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge be">SE</span></span><span>No income statement impact; retained earnings reduced by carrying amount</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 215</span><span>Presents the equity movements this Topic's transactions produce</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 480</span><span>Determines whether an instrument is even within equity's scope before this Topic's mechanics apply</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 718</span><span>Now governs equity-based payments to non-employees, since ASU 2018-07</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn"><div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary preview</div>
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Small stock dividend</div><div class="glossary-def">A stock dividend of generally less than 20–25% of previously outstanding shares, requiring fair-value capitalisation of retained earnings.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Spinoff</div><div class="glossary-def">A distribution of a subsidiary's shares to the parent entity's own shareholders, recorded at carrying amount under US GAAP.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Cost method (treasury stock)</div><div class="glossary-def">A method recording repurchased shares at their reacquisition cost, with subsequent reissuance gains/losses recognised within equity.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Additional paid-in capital (APIC)</div><div class="glossary-def">The equity caption absorbing amounts in excess of par value from share issuances, stock dividend capitalisations, and treasury stock transactions.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.asc505} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
